1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,475
Block three.

2
00:00:01,475 --> 00:00:05,900
Interactions, putting the pieces together, and how to search properly.

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:13,575
Interaction databases differ in how direct their evidence is, and the range is wider than most people assume.

4
00:00:13,575 --> 00:00:22,925
At the most direct end: a curated record of a physical interaction that somebody measured, by co-immunoprecipitation or two-hybrid or structure.

5
00:00:22,925 --> 00:00:35,500
At the least direct end: an association inferred from indirect signals — the two proteins are co-expressed, or co-mentioned in the literature, or their genes sit next to each other in many bacterial genomes.

6
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:41,300
IntAct, part of the IMEx consortium, holds curated experimental interactions.

7
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:51,200
STRING answers a deliberately broader question — which proteins are functionally associated — by combining experimental and predicted evidence into a single score.

8
00:00:51,200 --> 00:01:00,425
FunCoup, built here at Stockholm University, constructs a comparable network by a different route, and a later course in the programme covers it properly.

9
00:01:00,425 --> 00:01:03,050
The thing to carry away is this.

10
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:11,075
A line between two proteins in a network diagram can mean somebody watched them bind, or it can mean they appear in the same papers.

11
00:01:11,075 --> 00:01:15,300
Those are very different claims, and the picture looks identical.

12
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:19,000
Always check which you are looking at before you build on it.

13
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:29,850
Here is the complex itself, described as a curated record rather than as coordinates: two alpha-globin chains, two beta-globin chains, and four haem groups.

14
00:01:29,850 --> 00:01:39,250
That is precisely the same object that two H H B shows you as atoms, described instead as a composition — what it is made of, in what ratio.

15
00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:48,650
The Complex Portal curates these, and it is the right place to look when your question is "what is the actual functional unit?" rather than "where are the atoms?".

16
00:01:48,650 --> 00:01:53,800
For contrast, IntAct lists twenty interaction records for beta-globin.

17
00:01:53,800 --> 00:02:03,675
One of them is with haemoglobin subunit zeta, which is an embryonic chain — and that tells you something real: this protein's partners change over development.

18
00:02:03,675 --> 00:02:07,325
A single complex record would not have shown you that.

19
00:02:07,325 --> 00:02:13,650
It is worth pausing on why a complex needs its own record at all, when the structure already exists.

20
00:02:13,650 --> 00:02:18,525
A structure is one experiment on one sample under one set of conditions.

21
00:02:18,525 --> 00:02:26,350
The complex record is a statement about biology: this is the functional unit, these are its components, in these proportions.

22
00:02:26,350 --> 00:02:33,425
It is assembled from many structures and many papers, and it survives when any individual structure is superseded.

23
00:02:33,425 --> 00:02:39,100
That distinction matters whenever you are reasoning about function rather than geometry.

24
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:44,000
If you want to know what haemoglobin is, the complex record answers you.

25
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,775
If you want to know where its atoms are, the structure does.

26
00:02:48,375 --> 00:02:51,875
Here is the whole thread of the lecture in one picture.

27
00:02:51,875 --> 00:03:00,550
Each row is a different database's record of the same underlying biology — the gene, the transcript, the protein, the structure, the complex.

28
00:03:00,550 --> 00:03:06,325
And each one is reached from the row above it by following a cross-reference, not by searching again.

29
00:03:06,325 --> 00:03:10,675
That is the practical demonstration of the first of our two habits.

30
00:03:10,675 --> 00:03:13,275
You type a name once, at the top.

31
00:03:13,275 --> 00:03:16,325
Everything after that is navigation.

32
00:03:16,325 --> 00:03:25,500
The notebook fetches every one of these records live, so what you see on the slide is what the databases say today, not what they said when the slide was made.

33
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:30,850
If something has changed since this was recorded, the notebook will show you the change.

34
00:03:30,850 --> 00:03:35,200
The lab ends with a dossier of your own, for human myoglobin.

35
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:40,275
A practical point about searching that will save you a great deal of time.

36
00:03:40,275 --> 00:03:52,175
An everything-at-once search — the single box on a database's front page — returns genome records, gene records, transcript records, protein records and literature, mixed together in one list.

37
00:03:52,175 --> 00:03:58,000
You then have to sort them out by eye, and the categories are not always obvious from the titles.

38
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:06,025
The same words typed into NCBI Gene, or into UniProt specifically, give you a short list of exactly one kind of thing.

39
00:04:06,025 --> 00:04:12,250
So the habit is: decide which biological level you want *before* you search, not after.

40
00:04:12,250 --> 00:04:16,375
It turns a page of mixed results into a handful of candidates.

41
00:04:16,375 --> 00:04:21,125
There is a second reason to narrow before you search, beyond saving time.

42
00:04:21,125 --> 00:04:25,975
A specific collection lets you filter in ways the general search cannot.

43
00:04:25,975 --> 00:04:29,825
In NCBI Gene you can restrict to an organism.

44
00:04:29,825 --> 00:04:33,425
In UniProt you can restrict to reviewed entries.

45
00:04:33,425 --> 00:04:40,125
Those filters do not exist on the front page, because the front page does not know what kind of thing you are looking for.

46
00:04:40,125 --> 00:04:43,425
And a warning about the word "human" in a query.

47
00:04:43,425 --> 00:04:53,875
Typing it does not filter by organism — it searches for the word, which appears in the descriptions of records from every organism that has ever been compared to a human one.

48
00:04:53,875 --> 00:04:56,025
You will get zebrafish.

49
00:04:56,025 --> 00:04:58,600
Use the organism filter.

50
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:03,425
And here is the version of all this that you can actually repeat.

51
00:05:03,425 --> 00:05:12,275
NCBI's E-utilities, reached through Bio dot Entrez in Biopython; the UniProt REST API; the PDB REST API.

52
00:05:12,275 --> 00:05:19,450
All three let you fetch records from a script, and the course notebook builds the entire beta-globin dossier that way.

53
00:05:19,450 --> 00:05:23,200
Two advantages over clicking, and they are both large.

54
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:26,000
The first is reproducibility.

55
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,125
The script *is* the record of what you did.

56
00:05:29,125 --> 00:05:34,600
There is no ambiguity about which record you fetched or when, because the code says so.

57
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:36,675
The second is scale.

58
00:05:36,675 --> 00:05:40,950
The same four lines that fetch one protein fetch four hundred.

59
00:05:40,950 --> 00:05:50,025
Clicking does not scale at all, and the moment your question involves more than a handful of records, the script is not the harder route — it is the easier one.

60
00:05:50,625 --> 00:05:55,925
The literature, briefly, because you will search it constantly for the rest of your career.

61
00:05:55,925 --> 00:05:58,250
PubMed is the index.

62
00:05:58,250 --> 00:06:06,500
It is built on MEDLINE, which goes back to nineteen seventy-one, and it holds citations and abstracts — not full text.

63
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:12,975
PubMed Central is a different resource with a confusingly similar name: it holds free full text.

64
00:06:12,975 --> 00:06:15,000
It is much smaller.

65
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:22,975
Only a fraction of what PubMed indexes has a PMC copy, so finding a paper in PubMed does not mean you can read it.

66
00:06:22,975 --> 00:06:30,100
If there is no PMC copy and no institutional access, try bioRxiv or the other preprint servers.

67
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:38,100
Many authors now deposit a preprint to establish priority without waiting for peer review, and that copy is free to anybody.

68
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:43,625
It is not identical to the published version, which is worth remembering when you quote it.

69
00:06:44,225 --> 00:06:53,675
Boolean operators in a search box are exactly set operations, and if you picture them that way you will predict the result instead of being surprised by it.

70
00:06:53,675 --> 00:06:57,475
AND is the intersection — the overlap of the two sets.

71
00:06:57,475 --> 00:07:01,475
OR is the union, which is A plus B minus the overlap.

72
00:07:01,475 --> 00:07:06,050
Note the subtraction: records matching both are not counted twice.

73
00:07:06,050 --> 00:07:08,725
NOT is A minus the overlap.

74
00:07:08,725 --> 00:07:11,925
The numbers on this slide check out exactly.

75
00:07:11,925 --> 00:07:25,875
Four hundred and nine thousand five hundred and sixty-one equals two hundred and eighty-eight thousand five hundred and seventy-seven, plus a hundred and twenty-five thousand four hundred and forty-two, minus four thousand four hundred and fifty-eight.

76
00:07:25,875 --> 00:07:37,050
And two hundred and eighty-four thousand one hundred and nineteen equals two hundred and eighty-eight thousand five hundred and seventy-seven minus four thousand four hundred and fifty-eight.

77
00:07:37,050 --> 00:07:43,775
The notebook does that arithmetic in code, so you can see it is not approximately true but exactly true.

78
00:07:43,775 --> 00:07:48,450
The operator that catches people is NOT, because it is not symmetric.

79
00:07:48,450 --> 00:07:57,900
A NOT B and B NOT A are different queries with different answers, and if you have ever typed one meaning the other, you will have been quietly misled rather than given an error.

80
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:01,800
A smaller trap, and an extremely common one.

81
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:09,850
Two words typed with a space between them are treated as an AND: find records containing both, anywhere in the record, in any order.

82
00:08:09,850 --> 00:08:17,525
The same two words inside quotation marks are a phrase: find records where they appear together, in that order, adjacent.

83
00:08:17,525 --> 00:08:22,825
The difference in result size is usually large, and occasionally it is enormous.

84
00:08:22,825 --> 00:08:27,450
Before you press enter, predict which of the two will give fewer results.

85
00:08:27,450 --> 00:08:37,375
If your prediction is wrong, you have learned something about how the search engine works — which is useful, and much better learned now than in the middle of a systematic review.

86
00:08:37,375 --> 00:08:42,675
One more wrinkle, specific to PubMed and worth knowing because it surprises people.

87
00:08:42,675 --> 00:08:51,125
PubMed normally expands your terms: it maps them onto MeSH subject headings and searches for synonyms as well as the words you typed.

88
00:08:51,125 --> 00:08:55,975
That expansion is usually helpful and occasionally not what you wanted.

89
00:08:55,975 --> 00:08:59,225
Quotation marks switch the expansion off.

90
00:08:59,225 --> 00:09:06,125
So quoting a phrase does two things at once — it demands adjacency, and it disables the synonym mapping.

91
00:09:06,125 --> 00:09:13,675
If a quoted search returns far fewer results than you expected, that second effect is often the reason, not the first.

92
00:09:13,675 --> 00:09:17,750
The search details panel shows you what PubMed actually ran.

93
00:09:17,750 --> 00:09:24,250
Look at it when a result surprises you; it is the single most useful diagnostic the site offers.

94
00:09:24,850 --> 00:09:31,825
There is a second way into the literature, and pretending otherwise would be silly: you are all going to ask a chatbot.

95
00:09:31,825 --> 00:09:34,075
So let us do it properly.

96
00:09:34,075 --> 00:09:41,600
The useful thing to notice is that three different prompts get you three different kinds of answer, and only one of them is checkable.

97
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:47,250
Ask how intronic variants affect splicing in HBB and you get an explanation.

98
00:09:47,250 --> 00:09:55,400
That is worth something — it tells you the mechanism to look for and the words people use for it — but there is nothing in it you can verify.

99
00:09:55,400 --> 00:10:01,050
Ask for two primary studies that measured the effect and the answer has to name papers.

100
00:10:01,050 --> 00:10:04,150
Now it can be wrong in a way you can detect.

101
00:10:04,150 --> 00:10:16,775
Ask for the PMID, what was measured and which figure shows it, and you have three handles: an identifier that either resolves or does not, a method that either matches the claim or does not, and a figure to open.

102
00:10:16,775 --> 00:10:23,550
One more thing matters, and it is easy to forget to ask: what did the assistant actually have in front of it?

103
00:10:23,550 --> 00:10:31,125
Text it learned during training, a database record it retrieved just now, or a paper you pasted into the conversation.

104
00:10:31,125 --> 00:10:37,025
Those are three very different bases for an answer, and only you can tell which one you are getting.

105
00:10:37,625 --> 00:10:40,225
Here is the part people skip.

106
00:10:40,225 --> 00:10:44,300
Checking a citation answers the question "which paper is this?".

107
00:10:44,300 --> 00:10:48,675
Reading the paper answers "what does this paper actually support?".

108
00:10:48,675 --> 00:10:55,400
They are separate achievements, and doing the first one well feels so much like diligence that it is easy to stop there.

109
00:10:55,400 --> 00:10:58,025
The first check is mechanical.

110
00:10:58,025 --> 00:11:05,625
Does the title match, do the authors match, does the year match, does the DOI or the PMID resolve to that same record.

111
00:11:05,625 --> 00:11:07,225
Two minutes.

112
00:11:07,225 --> 00:11:10,150
The second check is the actual work.

113
00:11:10,150 --> 00:11:15,250
Which cells, which comparison, what was measured, and where in the paper is the number.

114
00:11:15,250 --> 00:11:22,200
The abstract will not do: abstracts are written to sell the result, and the Results section is written to report it.

115
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:28,850
And keep this sentence somewhere: a working link proves nothing whatsoever about the sentence sitting next to it.

116
00:11:28,850 --> 00:11:31,825
We will see exactly that in a moment.

117
00:11:32,425 --> 00:11:36,725
Both of these are documented cases, published in the literature.

118
00:11:36,725 --> 00:11:40,175
I am not telling you stories about what might go wrong.

119
00:11:40,175 --> 00:11:43,550
The first is a reference that does not exist.

120
00:11:43,550 --> 00:12:00,125
A group published the raw ChatGPT output they had generated in April twenty twenty-three, and its first reference was a twenty twenty-one review by Chen and colleagues on sunscreen and skin cancer — with a journal, a volume, an issue, page numbers and a PubMed identifier.

121
00:12:00,125 --> 00:12:03,100
Everything a reference normally has.

122
00:12:03,100 --> 00:12:05,425
The PMID is real.

123
00:12:05,425 --> 00:12:11,550
It belongs to a paper about iron-oxide microspheres for enriching histidine-rich proteins.

124
00:12:11,550 --> 00:12:15,175
Not the same field, not the same decade of your life.

125
00:12:15,175 --> 00:12:20,925
So a student who clicks the link, sees that it works, and moves on, has checked nothing at all.

126
00:12:20,925 --> 00:12:26,225
The second case is harder, because the paper is real and the citation is correct.

127
00:12:26,225 --> 00:12:33,375
A model was asked to summarise a named trial of saline against albumin in patients with traumatic brain injury.

128
00:12:33,375 --> 00:12:37,175
It reported no significant difference in mortality.

129
00:12:37,175 --> 00:12:45,825
The paper reports thirty-three point two per cent against twenty point four per cent at twenty-four months, with a p value of nought point nought nought three.

130
00:12:45,825 --> 00:12:48,900
The central finding had not been garbled.

131
00:12:48,900 --> 00:12:51,000
It had been deleted.

132
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:56,925
Let me finish this part with the opposite of a failure: a real paper, read properly.

133
00:12:56,925 --> 00:13:07,175
Nualkaew and colleagues, PMID thirty-one one one three nine nine six, tested an engineered U seven RNA against a splicing defect in beta-globin.

134
00:13:07,175 --> 00:13:17,100
The cells were erythroid cells cultured from patients carrying the variant, compared against untreated cells, a vector control and a scrambled-sequence control.

135
00:13:17,100 --> 00:13:27,425
The measurement was RT-PCR, in figure three, panels A and B, and the number is a mean of eight point two five per cent correctly spliced beta-globin RNA at day ten.

136
00:13:27,425 --> 00:13:32,000
Now read that number carefully, because it is a percentage of RNA.

137
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:37,975
It is not the percentage of cells that were corrected, and it is certainly not a treatment that works.

138
00:13:37,975 --> 00:13:47,500
The honest one-sentence version is: in cultured patient-derived cells carrying this variant, an engineered RNA partly restored correct splicing.

139
00:13:47,500 --> 00:13:51,175
Every qualifier in that sentence is doing a job.

140
00:13:51,175 --> 00:14:01,075
Take any one of them out and you have written something the experiment does not support — which, as we just saw, is the most common way a true statement becomes a false one.

141
00:14:01,675 --> 00:14:07,000
Which generalises into a habit worth having at every database, not just PubMed.

142
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:08,850
Predict the count.

143
00:14:08,850 --> 00:14:10,650
Run the query.

144
00:14:10,650 --> 00:14:13,175
Check that the answer makes sense.

145
00:14:13,175 --> 00:14:20,700
If you expected a few hundred and got two million, something is wrong with the query — not with the field, and not with biology.

146
00:14:20,700 --> 00:14:28,350
If you expected thousands and got six, likewise: probably a typo, a wrong field, or quotation marks you did not mean.

147
00:14:28,350 --> 00:14:35,550
The prediction costs five seconds and catches most mistakes before they turn into results you then have to explain.

148
00:14:35,550 --> 00:14:42,450
It is the same instinct as estimating an answer before doing the arithmetic, and it works for the same reason.

149
00:14:42,450 --> 00:14:48,475
This habit has a name in other fields: a sanity check, or an order-of-magnitude estimate.

150
00:14:48,475 --> 00:14:53,000
The reason it works is that errors in queries tend to be large errors.

151
00:14:53,000 --> 00:15:00,800
A misplaced quotation mark or a wrong field does not shift a count by ten per cent; it shifts it by a factor of a thousand.

152
00:15:00,800 --> 00:15:04,075
So even a very rough prediction catches them.

153
00:15:04,075 --> 00:15:07,325
It also protects against a subtler failure.

154
00:15:07,325 --> 00:15:18,600
If you run a query, get a plausible-looking number, and never form an expectation, you will accept whatever you get — including the result of a query that quietly did not mean what you thought.

155
00:15:18,600 --> 00:15:23,425
The prediction is what turns a silent wrong answer into a visible surprise.

156
00:15:24,025 --> 00:15:29,175
A short list of resources this course does not use, but which you will run into.

157
00:15:29,175 --> 00:15:36,750
They are here so that the names are familiar when they appear — in a paper, in a seminar, in somebody else's methods section.

158
00:15:36,750 --> 00:15:44,125
Variant databases, cancer genomics resources, proteomics repositories, pathway and model databases.

159
00:15:44,125 --> 00:15:48,025
Each one answers a question we do not ask in this course.

160
00:15:48,025 --> 00:15:50,750
You are not expected to learn them now.

161
00:15:50,750 --> 00:15:54,300
You are expected not to be startled by them later.

162
00:15:54,300 --> 00:15:58,400
Let me name a few, so that the words are not strange when you meet them.

163
00:15:58,400 --> 00:16:09,000
For variants: dbSNP catalogues them, ClinVar records their clinical interpretation, and the GWAS Catalog collects associations with traits.

164
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:12,075
For cancer: TCGA and COSMIC.

165
00:16:12,075 --> 00:16:14,425
For proteomics: PRIDE.

166
00:16:14,425 --> 00:16:18,475
For pathways and models: Reactome and BioModels.

167
00:16:18,475 --> 00:16:27,625
Each answers a question this course does not ask, and each has its own identifiers, its own conventions and its own notion of who vouches for a claim.

168
00:16:27,625 --> 00:16:35,650
When you do need one, the habits from today transfer unchanged — find out what kind of record you are holding, and what is behind it.

169
00:16:36,250 --> 00:16:41,125
The lab takes the same path on a different protein: human myoglobin.

170
00:16:41,125 --> 00:16:43,550
The same five questions.

171
00:16:43,550 --> 00:16:46,275
The same five kinds of record.

172
00:16:46,275 --> 00:16:51,225
Followed the same way, by cross-reference rather than by repeated searching.

173
00:16:51,225 --> 00:17:01,450
The one addition is that for each record you also have to say what kind of information it holds, and who vouches for it — submitted, predicted, curated or experimental.

174
00:17:01,450 --> 00:17:05,150
That last part is the actual point of the exercise.

175
00:17:05,150 --> 00:17:09,350
Fetching records is easy, and it gets easier every year.

176
00:17:09,350 --> 00:17:15,075
Knowing what you have fetched, and how much weight it will bear, is the skill that does not go out of date.

177
00:17:15,075 --> 00:17:19,550
Myoglobin is a deliberate choice rather than an arbitrary one.

178
00:17:19,550 --> 00:17:28,675
It is a globin, so everything you have learned about beta-globin transfers — the same families, the same kinds of record, the same structural literature.

179
00:17:28,675 --> 00:17:37,575
But it is a single chain rather than part of a tetramer, it carries oxygen in muscle rather than in blood, and its database footprint is smaller.

180
00:17:37,575 --> 00:17:45,300
So it is familiar enough that you are not starting from nothing, and different enough that you cannot simply copy the answers from the lecture.

181
00:17:45,300 --> 00:17:52,275
When you build the dossier, keep the accessions with their versions as you go, rather than reconstructing them at the end.

182
00:17:52,275 --> 00:17:55,400
Reconstructing is where mistakes happen.

183
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:02,350
One last set of numbers, because they have changed the field underneath us within your lifetimes as students.

184
00:18:02,350 --> 00:18:11,750
The Protein Data Bank holds about two hundred and sixty thousand measured structures, accumulated over more than fifty years of work by thousands of people.

185
00:18:11,750 --> 00:18:17,050
AlphaFold DB holds over two hundred and fourteen million predicted structures.

186
00:18:17,050 --> 00:18:21,500
The ESM Atlas holds over six hundred and seventeen million.

187
00:18:21,500 --> 00:18:23,275
Do the division.

188
00:18:23,275 --> 00:18:29,700
More than ninety-nine point nine per cent of the structures you can look up today were never measured by anybody.

189
00:18:29,700 --> 00:18:33,475
Which brings this session's question back with real force.

190
00:18:33,475 --> 00:18:36,375
Who vouches for a predicted record?

191
00:18:36,375 --> 00:18:39,250
Not a curator — nobody read it.

192
00:18:39,250 --> 00:18:42,250
Not an experiment — nothing was measured.

193
00:18:42,250 --> 00:18:46,100
A model vouches for it, with a confidence score attached.

194
00:18:46,100 --> 00:18:48,975
That is not a reason to distrust them.

195
00:18:48,975 --> 00:18:51,575
The good ones are genuinely good.

196
00:18:51,575 --> 00:18:57,850
It is a reason to know which kind of thing you are holding, which is what this entire session has been about.

197
00:18:57,850 --> 00:19:02,875
Session twelve is where we find out what that confidence score is actually worth.

198
00:19:03,475 --> 00:19:07,650
To close, the four habits this session has been building towards.

199
00:19:07,650 --> 00:19:10,075
None of them is difficult.

200
00:19:10,075 --> 00:19:15,350
All of them are the difference between work somebody else can repeat and work they cannot.

201
00:19:15,350 --> 00:19:16,300
One.

202
00:19:16,300 --> 00:19:21,325
Follow cross-references between records rather than searching again by name.

203
00:19:21,325 --> 00:19:22,250
Two.

204
00:19:22,250 --> 00:19:27,650
Report accession point version, and report coordinates with their genome build.

205
00:19:27,650 --> 00:19:28,625
Three.

206
00:19:28,625 --> 00:19:34,350
Ask where each claim comes from: submitted, predicted, curated or experimental.

207
00:19:34,350 --> 00:19:35,200
Four.

208
00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:38,100
Predict the count before you run the query.

209
00:19:38,100 --> 00:19:49,900
If you do those four things consistently, you will avoid most of the errors that this field actually makes — which are not sophisticated algorithmic errors, but fetching the wrong record and not noticing.

210
00:19:49,900 --> 00:19:54,050
I want to finish with the reason these habits matter more than they sound.

211
00:19:54,050 --> 00:19:59,100
The errors that damage computational biology are rarely sophisticated.

212
00:19:59,100 --> 00:20:05,050
They are not subtle bugs in alignment algorithms or deep misunderstandings of statistics.

213
00:20:05,050 --> 00:20:15,475
Overwhelmingly, they are somebody using the wrong record: the wrong transcript, the wrong genome build, the wrong organism, an annotation that was predicted and treated as measured.

214
00:20:15,475 --> 00:20:23,850
Those errors are invisible, because the analysis runs perfectly well on the wrong input and produces a confident, plausible answer.

215
00:20:23,850 --> 00:20:28,550
The four habits on this slide are cheap precisely because they are not clever.

216
00:20:28,550 --> 00:20:30,425
They are bookkeeping.

217
00:20:30,425 --> 00:20:36,075
And bookkeeping is what stands between a result that holds up and one that quietly does not.

218
00:20:36,675 --> 00:20:39,450
Next time: sequence alignment.

219
00:20:39,450 --> 00:20:50,300
Given two sequences, which residues correspond to which — and how do you find the best answer without trying every possibility, when the number of possibilities is astronomical?

